/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   ORIVEN AI MARKETING WORKSPACE — COMPLETE REDESIGN
   New architecture: Left sidebar + workflow-based navigation
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* Hide old top nav */
.ap-nav, #apNav, .ap-mob-nav, #apMobNav, #mobileBottomNav, .mbn { display: none !important; }

/* App shell: sidebar + content side by side */
html, body { overflow: hidden !important; height: 100vh !important; }
body { flex-direction: row !important; }
.app { display: flex !important; flex-direction: row !important; height: 100vh !important; overflow: hidden !important; }

/* Root-cause fix for "content shifted right" bugs across every route that
   uses this pattern (not a per-page hack): most routes live nested INSIDE
   .mc, which correctly fills the remaining width via its own flex:1. A
   number of .page elements (e.g. #page-campaigns, #page-campaign-results)
   are instead direct flex-item SIBLINGS of .mc right here in .app's row,
   and .page{} declares no width/flex of its own -- so when one becomes
   .active it shrink-to-fits its own content instead of stretching, while
   .mc (empty, since no page is active inside it) absorbs the leftover
   space in front of it, visually shoving the real content far to the
   right with a huge empty gap after the sidebar.
   This selector only matches .page elements that are ACTUALLY .app's
   direct children (the affected pattern) -- it does nothing to the many
   more routes correctly nested inside .mc, so it self-scopes to exactly
   the affected routes without needing a maintained list of page IDs. An
   overwhelming flex-grow (vs .mc's flex:1) makes the active page correctly
   claim the real available main-content width; each page's own inner
   max-width/margin:auto wrapper (e.g. .cgr-wrap) then centers properly
   within that correctly-sized box, exactly as it always intended to. */
/* Same root cause, second symptom: .app itself paints a legacy near-black
   background (.app{background:var(--ap-bg)}, --ap-bg:#09090B -- an old
   token whose real visual effect is "superseded by later var(--bg) rules"
   on every page that's correctly nested inside .mc, per that variable's
   own definition comment). A .page that's a direct .app child (this exact
   selector) has no opaque background of its own, so on any page whose
   content doesn't happen to fill 100% of the box (e.g. Drafts render two
   short rows, leaving empty space below), .app's dark legacy background
   -- and the always-running #appAmbient canvas beneath it -- shows
   through in the gap. Painting the real, current, theme-aware page
   background here (same var(--bg) token body/.mc already use) removes
   the gap for every affected page at once, not just one. */
.app > .page.active { flex: 9999 1 0%; width: auto; min-width: 0; max-width: 100%; background: var(--bg); }

/* Same root cause, third symptom (Autopilot only -- scoped narrowly rather
   than added to the shared rule above, since several other pages that match
   .app > .page.active already have their own working internal scroll
   solution and a blanket change here risks stacking a second scrollbar on
   top of theirs). #page-autopilot never inherits .mc's own overflow-y:auto
   (that lives only on .mc itself) and the base .page{} rule sets
   min-height:100% with no overflow of its own, so its real content -- which
   is taller than one viewport -- has nowhere to scroll: .app's
   overflow:hidden clips it outright with no scrollbar anywhere. min-height:0
   lets the flex item shrink to the row's cross-size instead of growing to
   fit its content, which is what makes the following overflow-y actually
   engage instead of never triggering. */
.app > #page-autopilot.active { min-height: 0; height: 100vh; overflow-y: auto; overflow-x: hidden; }

/* ── New sidebar ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
/* ROOT CAUSE (found via live pixel sampling on the actual rendered page,
   not just DOM geometry): this rule's background was `var(--bg, #0E0E0F)`
   -- but `--bg` (#F6F3EE in light mode) is the EXACT SAME variable body
   and .mc paint their own backgrounds with. So no matter how wide this
   box was made (220px -> 320px), the visible painted region never changed
   at all -- sidebar, page, and main content were all one indistinguishable
   flat color, with only a single 1px border line marking the edge. Every
   prior "make it wider" pass was correct about the box model and
   completely unable to produce a visible result, because the box was
   visually invisible to begin with. Fixed by giving the sidebar its own
   background token, already defined in the existing palette for exactly
   this "surface distinct from page background" role (--bg3), instead of
   inventing a new color. Dark mode gets the same treatment just below,
   since it has the identical bug (var(--bg) there also matches .mc). */
.orv-sb {
  display: flex !important;
  flex-direction: column !important;
  width: 280px !important;
  min-width: 280px !important;
  max-width: 280px !important;
  height: 100vh !important;
  background: var(--bg3, #E5DED4) !important;
  border-right: 1px solid var(--b2, rgba(0,0,0,.14)) !important;
  overflow: hidden !important;
  flex-shrink: 0 !important;
  position: relative !important;
  z-index: 9200 !important;
}
/* Dark-mode background/width for .orv-sb is NOT set here -- a later,
   higher-specificity `body.dark-mode .orv-sb` rule (search this file for
   "SIDEBAR V2") always wins over anything placed at this point in the
   cascade, so that's the one actually fixed for dark mode too. */
.orv-sb-logo {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 10px;
  padding: 16px 10px 14px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border, rgba(255,255,255,.07));
  flex-shrink: 0;
  cursor: default;
}
.orv-sb-logo-img { width: 40px; height: 40px; object-fit: contain; border-radius: 6px; }
.orv-sb-logo-name { font-size: 14px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--charcoal, #18181A); letter-spacing: -.02em; }
body.dark-mode .orv-sb-logo-name { color: rgba(255,255,255,.9); }
.orv-sb-nav {
  flex: 1;
  overflow-y: auto;
  overflow-x: hidden;
  padding: 12px 10px;
  scrollbar-width: none;
}
.orv-sb-nav::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }
.orv-sb-section {
  font-size: 10px;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .09em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: rgba(0,0,0,.3);
  padding: 14px 8px 6px;
}
body.dark-mode .orv-sb-section { color: rgba(255,255,255,.22); }
.orv-ni {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 10px;
  padding: 8px 10px;
  border-radius: 8px;
  cursor: pointer;
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 500;
  color: rgba(0,0,0,.5);
  transition: background .12s, color .12s;
  margin-bottom: 1px;
  border: none;
  background: none;
  width: 100%;
  text-align: left;
  position: relative;
  user-select: none;
}
.orv-ni:hover { background: rgba(0,0,0,.04); color: var(--charcoal, #18181A); }
body.dark-mode .orv-ni { color: rgba(255,255,255,.5); }
body.dark-mode .orv-ni:hover { background: rgba(255,255,255,.05); color: rgba(255,255,255,.9); }
.orv-ni.orv-active {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--green) 9%, transparent);
  color: var(--green-text);
  font-weight: 600;
}
body.dark-mode .orv-ni.orv-active { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--green) 10%, transparent); color: var(--green); }
.orv-ni.orv-active::before {
  content: '';
